\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 People are as concerned about the panopticon of ever-present surveillance as they are about the implications of an artificial intelligence sold to the public as destroying jobs if not the planet. But the two technology rollouts share something else in common: the rather sneaky ways in which they are being foisted on the public.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 City officials repeatedly are signing nondisclosure agreements with developers and concealing the details about data center development from the public. Flock cameras have also appeared without public deliberation or warning in a lot of communities, but the bigger issue is that municipalities are purchasing the equipment with money originally intended for other purposes.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 In both cases, the secrecy and underhandedness give the impression that public officials value tech partnerships more than transparency, and that there really is something to hide with these systems. People clearly want a voice in deciding the future of their communities and the terms upon which that future will proceed. In this sense, data centers and Flock cameras are in some ways physical manifestations of the nation\u8217 s soaring inequality, an egregious example of how economic power gets translated into political power. Shady closed-door deals just make that perception more egregious.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 Diverting settlement funds for other uses is a favorite pastime of the powerful. In the aftermath of the foreclosure fraud settlement, states stole the money to fill budget holes or other purposes rather than help victims of illegal foreclosures. The latest pot of cash that states can raid comes from the national opioid settlement. More than $50 billion was made available to heal communities destroyed by the epidemic, expected to be spent on initiatives like treatment and detox centers and anti-overdose medications.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 But as a KFF investigation reveals, a nontrivial amount of these settlement funds has gone into policing for things like Tasers and drones. And since law enforcement loves nothing more than purchasing new toys, that also means that opioid settlement dollars are buying Flock cameras. Mother Jones was able to find 19 agencies in ten states that have spent close to $1 million on ALPRs; the total number is likely much higher.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 Police departments have justified this by saying that ALPRs can fight crime, and drug crime in particular, and therefore will prevent epidemics from recurring. But that is manifestly not the purpose of the opioid settlement, which was to get help for addicted people who needed it. Incredibly, seven of the 19 expenditures on ALPRs came out of West Virginia, ground zero of the opioid crisis.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 That may not be the most outrageous Flock financing scheme. In Texas, the legislature imposed a new $1 fee on auto insurance, they said to combat the persistent stealing of catalytic converters, which is real (says this victim of catalytic converter theft). But $30 million in revenues from that fee (out of a total of $81 million) has been diverted toward purchasing and installing Flock cameras along state highways, according to The Texas Tribune. That\u8217 s 3,200 Flock cameras coming from the auto insurance fee.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 Again, you can twist yourself into saying that license plate readers can stop catalytic converter theft. But exactly how do Flock cameras on the }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 highway}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1  do that? I don\u8217 t think the thieves are wrenching these things out of the undersides of vehicles operating at top speed.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 Hilariously, when the Tribune asked Gov. Greg Abbott about this multiple times, he didn\u8217 t respond but then announced that the state would pause all funding for Flock cameras.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}
\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf1 People are definitely angry about the all-seeing, all-knowing aspect of Flock cameras, and the easy opportunities for abuse, from the mundanity of rogue police officers using them to spy on their ex-girlfriends (something we hear about seemingly daily), or the more invasive collaborations with ICE to find immigrants, or even searches to find women who got abortions. But I would argue that the bait and switch is a critical angle as well.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 In the Trump era, people\u8217 s radar for corruption is heightened. The combination of not wanting Flock cameras in their communities and local and state governments raiding money earmarked for other purposes to buy them is magnifying the frustration. If these are truly benign technologies that help stop crime, people reasonably think, why are they being secretly funded?}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 The lesson of Watergate was that it\u8217 s not the crime but the cover-up. Flock cameras would capture attention even if municipalities were deliberately installing them after public debate. But the cover-up is just heightening the anger.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 The post How You\u8217 re Paying for All These Flock Cameras appeared first on The American Prospect.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0
